= Staal's Influence

Although Staal insists he has no intention of establishing a military dictatorship and claims the Commonwealth Army is merely stabilizing the nation in its time of need, we suspect he is maneuvering to consolidate power. This, of course, involves the infiltration of his supporters into the Greater Worker's Guild and the quiet persuasion of <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> leaders through shady deals and under-the-table arrangements. It is already evident that our decision-making is being compromised.

The longer we watch him work, the harder it is to believe that "stabilizing the nation" is the whole of it. Staal is not making the Commonwealth strong so that it can stand on its own. He is making it strong and orderly enough to be worth something at a bargaining table, and there is only one table he can mean. He has never said the word, and he probably never will, but all his talk of strength and order points north, toward the Empress's court and the reunion the old republic was founded to refuse.

What unsettles us most is how little the prospect alarms anyone else. A decade of crisis has worn the country down, and to a great many Commonwealthers a return to an Empire that promised an end to the chaos no longer sounds like surrender but like relief. This is Staal's advantage: he frightens no one. He is not Gallax, raving of purges and crusades. He is the reasonable general who will work with anyone but the <span style="color: #700000;">Collectivists</span> and asks only for calm. The committed democrats among us see this plainly and say so to anyone who will listen. Staal is gentler than Gallax, but the destination is the same, and our democracy will not survive the journey under either of them.

- @root: Ok.
